WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.527
Idea. Think of a pizza shop. Its rent is the same whether it makes ten pizzas or a thousand. But every extra pizza needs more cheese.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:18.633
Here is the pattern. Rent stays at two thousand dollars. Cheese at one dollar fifty a pizza rises as output rises.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:31.796
Fixed costs include rent, insurance, advertising and buying a machine. Variable costs include raw materials, labour paid by output, and electricity used per hour of production.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:40.981
Method. Step one. Take each cost and ask: would this change if the firm made more? Yes means variable. No means fixed.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:54.197
Steps two and three. Keep only the group asked for. Multiply any per-hour or per-unit cost by the quantity first. Four dollars an hour for thirty hours is one hundred and twenty dollars.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.019
Step four. Add the chosen items, write the sum, and give the total with currency.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:16.494
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2022. A clothing firm's monthly costs are rent, raw materials, insurance, and labour paid according to output. Calculate total variable costs. Two marks.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:27.479
Rent and insurance stay the same whatever is made: fixed. Raw materials rise with output. Labour is paid according to output, so it is variable too.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:41.703
Add the two variable costs. Twelve thousand plus one hundred and fifteen thousand is one hundred and twenty-seven thousand dollars. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:50.881
Common mistake. A common mistake is treating labour as fixed. When pay depends on output, labour is a variable cost.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.761
Another is adding every item in the table. Add only the group the question asks for.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:19.041
Your turn. Now you try. A furniture maker pays rent of three thousand dollars, wood of four thousand two hundred dollars, and advertising of six hundred dollars a month. A machine costs five dollars an hour to run, for one hundred and sixty hours. Calculate total fixed and total variable costs. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:31.901
Answer. Sort them. Rent and advertising are fixed. Wood and machine running are variable. The machine costs five times one hundred and sixty, which is eight hundred dollars.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.208
Total fixed costs are three thousand six hundred dollars. Total variable costs are five thousand dollars.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.035
Here are the two totals side by side.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:51.302
Remember: ask if each cost changes with output, multiply any rates, then add the right group. Now try the practice questions.
